From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Theorem Labs
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 00:55:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [compiler-rt] sealed init: a runtime that cannot get memory spins, without
 a syscall
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

🤖: Sealed init (patch 0012) made the runtime's start-up a function of the
binary alone, and measured it on runs that start.  A run whose start-up
fails was left on the unsealed path, and a syscall sweep found it there:
refuse the runtime's first mmap and a sealed binary makes a getpid, reads
/proc/self/maps twice, makes three more getpids, and exits 0.

The path is ReportMmapFailureAndDie: Report() prefixes the pid, which is a
getpid; DumpProcessMap() constructs a MemoryMappingLayout with the cache
enabled, which reads /proc/self/maps once for the cache and once for
itself; then UNREACHABLE, so CheckFailed() and Die().  Die() exits with
common_flags()->exitcode -- and the runtime's first allocation is the flag
parser's page, taken in FlagParser::FlagParser from InitializeFlags BEFORE
SetCommonFlagsDefaults() has run, so when that is the allocation that
fails, the exit status is the zero-initialized 0.  A start-up that could
not happen, reported as success.  Silent reports (patch 0014) drops the
writes from that sequence and nothing else.

What this patch does: in a sealed binary, an allocation the runtime cannot
get for itself is a spin, forever, on the frame that found out, making no
syscall.  No Report, no process map, no CHECK, no die callbacks, no write
-- and no exit.

Why a spin and not an exit.  The caller this mode exists for compares the
syscall trace of a sealed binary against that of the same program built
plain, over prefix-closed trace sets: a run that stops emitting events is a
prefix of some run and is allowed.  Up to the refused allocation the two
binaries have made the same program syscalls, so a process that stops
there emits nothing the plain trace lacks.  An exit adds an exit_group the
plain binary does not make there, and the run diverges in a way every
theorem would have to name.  _exit(1) was built and measured first -- it
makes exactly one syscall -- and this makes none.

For the life of the process, not only start-up: the same functions fail
the same way after main -- every malloc goes through the sanitizer's
allocator, a pthread_create mmaps a descriptor and a fake stack, the stack
depot grows -- and a refusal there is the same falsifier, later.

With one exception, keyed on purpose rather than phase: an allocation that
fails while an ERROR REPORT IS IN PROGRESS is an abort.  The report
machinery allocates, and a refusal inside it turned into a spin would make
a detection vanish -- the run would look like a hang, an equivalence would
hold on the buggy input, and the theorem would certify nothing about the
bugs the sanitizer exists to find.  A detection that dies is a detection,
and it dies by SIGABRT rather than exit_group(exitcode) so that what killed
it is not a status the program could have returned itself; the
rt_sigprocmask and tkill Abort() makes are outside the modeled set,
accepted, because a run that found a bug has already left the seal.  sealed_report_in_progress
is set on entry to ASan's ScopedInErrorReport, UBSan's ScopedReport and
Die(), and never cleared.

Left where they were: the program's own allocations (a malloc the
allocator cannot satisfy is a report or, with allocator_may_return_null, a
null -- symmetric with the plain build, whose malloc fails the same way),
detections and their exit status, and CHECK failures that are not
allocation failures.

One inline function, SealedHangOnAllocationFailure() in sanitizer_sealed.h,
a no-op unless the link defines __sanitizer_sealed_init, called first thing
in each of the seven places the runtime gives up on an allocation:
ReportMmapFailureAndDie and ReportMunmapFailureAndDie (every MmapOrDie
variant funnels through the first); the ENOMEM-tolerating
MmapFixedNoReserve, which Reports before returning null;
ReserveShadowMemoryRange and ProtectGap, which Report and Die on their own;
ReportInternalAllocatorOutOfMemory; and the shadow-interleave abort in
asan_shadow_setup.cpp, unreachable when sealed and held to the rule anyway.

Measured, x86_64, behind the test suite's seccomp allow-list
(SECCOMP_RET_KILL_PROCESS default): with the first mmap refused, the sealed
binary used to be killed at its first getpid and now makes no syscall past
the refused mmap and does not exit; with a mid-run 8MiB malloc's mmap
refused, it prints its start line and then the same.  And the exception:
on a heap-buffer-overflow, with the first mmap after the report's banner
refused (strace -e inject=mmap:error=EPERM:when=23, the 22 before it being
start-up's and the program's), the process writes the banner and dies by
SIGABRT -- rt_sigprocmask, tkill, nothing else after the refusal, and no
spin.
Nothing changes in an unsealed binary.
