 inline bool SealedInit() { return &__sanitizer_sealed_init != nullptr; }
 
+// What a sealed binary does when the runtime cannot get memory for itself:
+// nothing, forever.  It spins, on the frame that found out, making no syscall.
+//
+// The unsealed runtime answers the same failure with its report machinery.
+// ReportMmapFailureAndDie is the shape of it: Report(), which prefixes the pid
+// and so is a getpid; DumpProcessMap(), which is two reads of /proc/self/maps
+// (MemoryMappingLayout with cache_enabled reads once to fill the cache and once
+// for itself) and an MmapOrDie of its own; then UNREACHABLE, so CheckFailed()
+// and Die(), which runs the die callbacks and exits with common_flags()->
+// exitcode.  Every one of those is a syscall the seal promised not to make,
+// and one of them is wrong as well as unsealed: the runtime's first allocation
+// is the flag parser's page (FlagParser::FlagParser, from InitializeFlags),
+// which is taken BEFORE SetCommonFlagsDefaults() runs, so when that is the
+// allocation that fails, Die() reads the zero-initialized exitcode and the
+// process exits 0 -- a start-up that could not happen, reported as success.
+// Measured, x86_64, first mmap refused with EPERM: getpid, two openat of
+// /proc/self/maps, three more getpids, exit_group(0).
+//
+// Why a spin and not an exit.  The caller this mode exists for compares the
+// syscall trace of a sealed binary against the trace of the same program
+// built plain, and its trace sets are prefix-closed: a run that stops emitting
+// events partway through is a prefix of some run, and is allowed.  Up to the
+// refused allocation the two binaries have made the same program syscalls
+// (the runtime's own mmaps are excused as the runtime's), so a process that
+// stops there emits nothing the plain trace does not have.  An exit does not
+// stop there: exit_group is an event, it is one the plain binary does not
+// make at that point, and the run diverges in a way the model would have to
+// name -- a case split over every theorem, for a run in which nothing about
+// the program was learned.  A spin is the one answer whose trace contribution
+// is empty.  _exit(1) was the alternative that was built first, and it was
+// measured to make exactly one syscall; this makes none.
+//
+// It applies for the life of the process, not only during start-up.  The
+// same functions fail the same way later: every malloc the program makes goes
+// through the sanitizer's allocator, which mmaps regions and large chunks on
+// demand; a pthread_create mmaps a thread descriptor and a fake stack; the
+// stack depot and quarantine grow.  A refusal there is the same falsifier as
+// one at start-up, later -- an exit the plain binary does not make -- and the
+// prefix argument is the same, so the answer is the same.
+//
+// The one exception, and it matters more than when the failure happens: an
+// allocation that fails while an ERROR REPORT IS IN PROGRESS is an abort.
+// The report machinery allocates -- the stack depot, symbolization, the
+// internal allocator for the report's own bookkeeping -- and if the sanitizer
+// finds a real bug, starts reporting, and a refusal inside that path turned
+// into a spin, the detection would vanish: the run would look like a hang the
+// plain build also could have, an equivalence would hold on the buggy input,
+// and the theorem would certify nothing about the bugs the sanitizer exists
+// to find.  A detection that dies is a detection; a detection that spins is
+// not.  And it dies by SIGABRT rather than by exit_group(exitcode) so that
+// what killed it is not a status the program itself could have returned: a
+// caller who sees the death sees a report that could not finish, not a
+// program that chose 1.  What Abort() does to get there -- an
+// rt_sigprocmask and a tkill -- is outside the modeled set, and that is
+// accepted: this is a run that has already left the seal by finding a bug.  So the rule is keyed on purpose, not on phase.
+// sealed_report_in_progress is set on the way into every report scope --
+// ASan's ScopedInErrorReport, UBSan's ScopedReport -- and on entry to Die(),
+// which is where every other report path ends up, and it is never cleared:
+// once a process has started reporting, its next internal allocation failure
+// is an abort.
+//
+// Two boundaries deliberately left where they were.  The program's own
+// allocations: a malloc the sanitizer's allocator cannot satisfy with ENOMEM
+// is answered as it always was -- a report (ReportOutOfMemory) or, with
+// allocator_may_return_null, a null the program handles -- because that
+// failure is symmetric with the plain build's, whose malloc fails the same
+// way, and a spin there would create an asymmetry where none exists.  And
+// detections: the report path, its exit status, and everything the
+// silent-reports mode (sanitizer_silent.h) governs are untouched.  Nor does
+// any of this cover CHECK failures that are not allocation failures.
+//
+// The loop is a compiler barrier and nothing else: no sched_yield (a syscall),
+// no pause instruction needed (nothing is waiting on it), and the asm
+// statement is what keeps a compiler from treating a side-effect-free infinite
+// loop as unreachable.
+//
+// Called first thing in each place the runtime gives up on an allocation:
+// ReportMmapFailureAndDie and ReportMunmapFailureAndDie (every MmapOrDie
+// variant funnels through the first); the ENOMEM-tolerating MmapFixedNoReserve
+// in sanitizer_posix_libcdep.cpp, which Reports before it returns null;
+// ReserveShadowMemoryRange and ProtectGap, which Report and Die on their own;
+// the internal allocator's out-of-memory path; and the shadow-interleave
+// abort in asan_shadow_setup.cpp, which sealed mode cannot reach but is held
+// to the same rule so that it is not an exception waiting to become one.  It
+// is a no-op in an unsealed binary.
+extern atomic_uint32_t sealed_report_in_progress;
+void NORETURN Abort();  // sanitizer_common.h, which this header does not include
+
+inline void SealedEnterReport() {
+  if (SealedInit())
+    atomic_store(&sealed_report_in_progress, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
+}
+
+inline void SealedHangOnAllocationFailure() {
+  if (!SealedInit())
+    return;
+  if (atomic_load(&sealed_report_in_progress, memory_order_relaxed))
+    Abort();
+  for (;;) __asm__ __volatile__("" ::: "memory");
+}
